  "query_preview": "..\n(Friday through Monday)\nIssued at 320 AM CST Tue Nov 10 2026\n\nCool, near-normal temperatures will occur on Friday as Oklahoma \nand north Texas remain under the influence of a surface high that \nremains in place across the Midwest, which will result in light \neast-to-northeast flow (continuing the cold air advection). \nTemperatures should see a slight moderation on Saturday with a \nreturn to southerly winds. However, increasing cloud cover ahead \nof the next system will likely temper the warmup.\n\nBy late Saturday into Sunday, a shortwave trough will lift into \nthe Plains, bringing a chance of rain. Elevated instability will \nbe present, so a few embedded thunderstorms are possible. There is\nthe potential for appreciable rainfall, with a 20 to 40% chance \nof receiving half an inch of rainfall. Depending on how heavy and \nwidespread the rainfall is, Sunday could be a chilly day. Bias-\ncorrected probabilistic guidance has a wide range of high \ntemperatures from the upper 40s to the low 70s deg F (10th to 90th\npercentile), with the cooler end of guidance more likely if the \nrainfall/cloud cover is widespread enough. If the rain/cloud cover\nis more limited, the warmer end of guidance would occur.\n\nFor next week (especially the latter half of the week), \ndeterministic models and their ensemble members indicate an \nincreasing probability of a more active weather pattern, with \nsouthwest flow developing across the southwestern United States. \nAs waves embedded within the southwest flow lift northeastward \ninto the Plains, there will be chances for showers and \nthunderstorms. This pattern also appears unfavorable for any\nsignificant cooldown.\n\nMahale",
